title: Loading a shift register
mode: vhdl
chapter: Memory
check: behaves
cycles: 150
random: load=0.2
---- intro
A shift register can also be **loaded** with a whole number at once: when `load` is 1, `s` takes `d`; otherwise it shifts left with `din` coming in at the bottom.

Data can then come in one bit at a time and go out as a whole number, or the other way round: load a number and send it out bit by bit, from the top.
---- task
At each rising edge: if `load` is 1, `s` takes `d`; else it shifts left with `din` coming in at bit 0. `q` shows `s`.
---- start
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity shift4_load is
  port(
    clk  : in  std_logic;
    load : in  std_logic;
    d    : in  std_logic_vector(3 downto 0);
    din  : in  std_logic;
    q    : out std_logic_vector(3 downto 0)
  );
end shift4_load;

architecture rtl of shift4_load is
    signal s : std_logic_vector(3 downto 0) := (others => '0');
begin
    -- write it here
end rtl;
---- answer
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity shift4_load is
  port(
    clk  : in  std_logic;
    load : in  std_logic;
    d    : in  std_logic_vector(3 downto 0);
    din  : in  std_logic;
    q    : out std_logic_vector(3 downto 0)
  );
end shift4_load;

architecture rtl of shift4_load is
    signal s : std_logic_vector(3 downto 0) := (others => '0');
begin
    process (clk)
    begin
        if rising_edge(clk) then
            if load = '1' then
                s <= d;
            else
                s <= s(2 downto 0) & din;
            end if;
        end if;
    end process;
    q <= s;
end rtl;
---- hint1
Inside the clock edge, an `if load = '1' then ... else ... end if;` with the shift in the `else`.
---- hint2
`if load = '1' then s <= d; else s <= s(2 downto 0) & din; end if;`, and `q <= s;` after the process.
---- solution
```
process (clk)
begin
    if rising_edge(clk) then
        if load = '1' then
            s <= d;
        else
            s <= s(2 downto 0) & din;
        end if;
    end if;
end process;
q <= s;
```
---- done
A serial port works like this: load a byte, then shift it out one bit per tick on a single wire. The other end shifts it back in.
